A||N||8||Justin||Turning a normal Fiero into a GT...||||1||
Z||000000||Justin||07-13-2001||10:44 AM||||I am looking to buy my first Fiero.  But if I can't find a GT that I like, well, I'm not getting one.  The thing for me that sets apart the GT from an SE or just the standard Fiero is the fastback design.  I like how the sail window frame thingys flow back instead of drop straight down at an angle like the notch backs.  So would it be possible to replace the notchback panels with the fast back panels?  If so, I couls start looking at SE's too, so that I could just switch the panels. I would take an SE, but not the normal Fiero becasue the front end of the SE and GT are really cool, but the front end of the others looks very ugly in my opinion, which is mainly because of the huge black bumpers and how it sits like 1 foot off the ground.  I would just get the GT, but right now I just want a good running Fiero with low miles, and I've seen at least 3 SE's that meet that description, but no GT's (for my price range, which is under $4,000).  What are the other differences between the GT and SE?  Any performance or body changes?  I want a 4 or 5 speed too, that seems like it would be more fun.  And I would really like a 6 cyl.  So is switching the fastback and notchback panels possible?<P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.plauder-smilies.de/poke2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Justin||07-13-2001||10:50 AM||||Well I just looked through the gallery and it appears I would need to replace the entire rear end to get the GT look.  That seems like it would be a bit more money then I'm willing to spend (anyone have an estimeate?).  Well I guess I will continue looking for GT's, maybe somedday one will pop up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.plauder-smilies.de/poke2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Master_Sushi||07-13-2001||11:07 AM||||The rear clips (notchback and fastback) are just one piece, the front nose is just bolt on. I have seen a few web pages that show the transplant with detailed instructions. I'm sure someone on the forum has them bookmarked or something. I have to take the rear clip off my 84SE project soon to do some restoration work. I don't think it will be that hard to do. I think whats involved is mostly time. Just like all fiero bodywork.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>Someone please correct me if Im wrong about anything!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||07-13-2001||11:34 AM||||I was in the same dilema as you. Was looking for a GT also (88) but they are more expensive. I found an excellent Formula which will become a GT in the future. Yes panels are interchangeable. Kickhill list the aero package fro $500-$650 and the GT rear clip for &gt;$500. So for $1000-$1500 & your labor you can convert it. In my case it will be way bellow the price of an 88 GT. Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Justin||07-13-2001||12:07 PM||||What's the aero package? I will not need a front bumper, the one I like is on the SE.  I will need the entire rear end from the doors back the way it seems.  Bumper, sail window frame thingy (what is that called anyway?), new rear lights, and anything else that goes behind the doors.  That's what I have come up with.  Is this correct?  If it's gonna cost over $1000, I might as well forget it.  I thought those plastic parts would be cheap.  It is plastic right?<P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<P>visit <A HREF="http://www.twinkiesproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twinkiesproject.com</A>  for information on the chemical properties, reactions, gravitational resonse, and density issues of a Twinkie.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||07-13-2001||12:47 PM||||You can find a used pair of GT tail lights for 150-200 bucks. <P>I know where a GT clip with no Qtr. windows can be found for 100 bucks but you must pick it up.  Its in South West Maryland.  <P>Qtr. windows you can get for $200 from Fiero Wharehouse/Doug K., top notch repro's.<P>Decklid too, $150 for one in same place as rear clip. I don't think you will need a bumper.  You will need the GT tail light wiring harness though, probably go to kick hill for that.  And the GT trunk seal, goes for $65 at gmpartsdirect.com last time I checked.  <P>Here is what it will look like naked, found at <A HREF="http://www.fiero.net" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.net</A> <P> <IMG SRC="http://fiero.cnchost.com/showroom/86dis6.jpg"> <BR> <BR>Hope this helps<P>  <P>||||reg||2
Z||000006||Alex4mula||07-13-2001||01:27 PM||||Like Black88GT said you won't need the rear bumper if you have an SE. The aero package is the lower look of the SE (front/rear bumpers and side rocker panels).<P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
